---
title: Keep Generated Files (Advanced)
id: keep-generated-files
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider URLs for generated media expire. A Sora clip, a batch of images, a long\naudio track: the model hands you a URL that stops working after a while, and\nonce it does the output is gone. To keep the output, save the generated bytes to\nyour own storage and serve them from your own origin, where they outlive the\nprovider's link."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is a server-side opt-in that layers on "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"top of"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" store\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" already requires. Byte storage\nadds two more stores, which must be provided together:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" store: the metadata."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":" store: the bytes."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What each choice gets you:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both provided"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" writes each generated file's\nbytes to the blob store, records an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArtifactRecord"},{"type":"text","value":", and attaches durable\nreferences to the result and the run record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Neither provided"}]},{"type":"text","value":": only the run record is kept."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryPersistence()"},{"type":"text","value":" ships all three stores ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":"), so it works out of the box. Any backend that implements "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArtifactStore"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":" (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-generation-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a generation adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":")\nworks the same way."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"serve-the-stored-bytes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serve the stored bytes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The bytes land under the blob key "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>"},{"type":"text","value":". To fetch a\ngenerated file later (to render it, download it, or hand it to another request),\nadd a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" route that reads the artifact back with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retrieveArtifact"},{"type":"text","value":" /\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retrieveBlob"},{"type":"text","value":" helpers and streams it from your own origin:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/api.generate.image.ts, runs the generation.\nimport {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  retrieveArtifact,\n  retrieveBlob,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest(\n    'image',\n    request,\n  )\n\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  // Persistence requires the scope these runs are filed under.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [\n      withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n        // Stamp the durable serve URL (the artifact route below) onto every\n        // persisted artifact ref, and rewrite the live result's media field to\n        // it. Both the live and the restored result then render from your own\n        // origin instead of the provider's expiring link.\n        artifactUrl: (ref) =>\n          `/api/generate/image/artifact?id=${ref.artifactId}`,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The serve route is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"separate"}]},{"type":"text","value":" route from the generation endpoint. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" on\nthe generation route is already spoken for by\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which is where a reloading\nclient hydrates and where an in-flight run resumes, so the bytes get their own\npath, the one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" stamps above:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// routes/api.generate.image.artifact.ts, serves stored bytes by id.\n//\n// This is a plain file endpoint: it serves one stored file, it does not resume\n// a run or rebuild a conversation.\n//\n// Security: the id comes from the caller, so this route MUST authorize before\n// it serves. `ArtifactRecord` carries the `threadId` / `runId` the file was\n// generated under. Check that against an identity you derive server-side from\n// the session, never from the query string. Without this check, any caller who\n// learns or guesses an artifact id can read another user's media.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const artifactId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('id')\n  if (!artifactId) return new Response('missing id', { status: 400 })\n\n  const artifact = await retrieveArtifact(persistence, artifactId)\n  if (!artifact) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  // Replace with your session + ownership check, e.g.:\n  // const user = await auth(request)\n  // const owned = user != null && (await db.threadOwnedBy(user.id, artifact.threadId))\n  const owned = true\n  void request\n  // 404, not 403. A distinguishable \"exists but forbidden\" confirms valid ids.\n  if (!owned) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(persistence, artifact)\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  return new Response(blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer()), {\n    headers: {\n      'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n      'content-length': String(artifact.size),\n    },\n  })\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"serve-video-honour-range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Serve video: honour "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Range"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The route above is enough for images. Video is not: seeking a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<video>"},{"type":"text","value":" is\nbuilt on HTTP range requests, and a source that answers every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Range"},{"type":"text","value":" with the\nwhole file cannot be scrubbed: Safari refuses to play it at all, and every\nother browser downloads the entire clip before it starts. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"range"},{"type":"text","value":" to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retrieveBlob"},{"type":"text","value":" and answer "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"206"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { parseRangeHeader } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ArtifactRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\n// The same route, seek-aware. `parseRangeHeader` resolves the header against\n// the size on the record, including suffix ranges (`bytes=-500` is the LAST\n// 500 bytes) and the unsatisfiable case. `blob.range` then reports the slice\n// actually served, and `artifact.size` the whole file: the two numbers\n// `Content-Range` needs.\nexport async function serveArtifactBytes(\n  request: Request,\n  artifact: ArtifactRecord,\n) {\n  const range = parseRangeHeader(request.headers.get('range'), artifact.size)\n  if (range === 'unsatisfiable') {\n    return new Response('range not satisfiable', {\n      status: 416,\n      headers: { 'content-range': `bytes */${artifact.size}` },\n    })\n  }\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(\n    persistence,\n    artifact,\n    range ? { range } : undefined,\n  )\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const body = blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer())\n  // `accept-ranges` on every response, including the whole-file one: it is how\n  // the player learns it may seek at all.\n  const headers = {\n    'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n    'accept-ranges': 'bytes',\n  }\n  if (!blob.range) {\n    return new Response(body, {\n      headers: { ...headers, 'content-length': String(artifact.size) },\n    })\n  }\n  const { offset, length } = blob.range\n  return new Response(body, {\n    status: 206,\n    headers: {\n      ...headers,\n      'content-length': String(length),\n      'content-range': `bytes ${offset}-${offset + length - 1}/${artifact.size}`,\n    },\n  })\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client side needs nothing special, which is the point. Given a route that\nanswers ranges, the browser drives the rest:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { PersistedArtifactRef } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function GeneratedVideo({ artifact }: { artifact: PersistedArtifactRef }) {\n  // `artifact.url` is the app-origin URL `artifactUrl` stamped on the ref.\n  return <video src={artifact.url} controls preload=\"metadata\" />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"preload=\"metadata\""},{"type":"text","value":" has the player fetch the header bytes with a range request\nand show the duration and scrubber without pulling the clip down. A store must\nsupport ranged reads for any of this to work; the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./store-reference#blobstore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"conformance testkit"}]},{"type":"text","value":" asserts it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps everything in process memory, which is right for\ndevelopment and tests; point "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":" at a durable backend\nfor production. Control what gets captured with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":"'s\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractArtifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" (return your own descriptors) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nameArtifact"},{"type":"text","value":" (name each\nfile) options."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractArtifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the built-in rule. If you use a video\nprovider with "},{"type":"link","href":"../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no public URL"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\nreturn a descriptor with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bytes: result.body"},{"type":"text","value":" for the video. If you do not, the\nstream is not stored and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"url"},{"type":"text","value":" is a base64 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data:"},{"type":"text","value":" URL."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-where-the-bytes-land","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose where the bytes land"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default an artifact's bytes are written under\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"storageKey"},{"type":"text","value":" to put them in your own\nfolder structure instead, which helps when the bucket is shared with the rest of\nyour app, or when you want media grouped by the thing it belongs to rather than\nby the run that produced it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const storageKeyOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  storageKey: ({ runId, artifactId, role, name }) =>\n    `products/${role}/${runId}-${artifactId}-${name}`,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two things worth knowing:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolved key is recorded on the artifact."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Once the path is arbitrary it\ncan no longer be recomputed from the record, so it is stored as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArtifactRecord.blobKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and reads resolve through it. Records written before\nthis existed fall back to the default convention, so adding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"storageKey"},{"type":"text","value":" to an\napp with existing artifacts does not orphan them. It does mean the default\nconvention can never be changed retroactively."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returning a non-unique key overwrites."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactId"},{"type":"text","value":", or something\nequally unique, unless overwriting is what you want."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is server-side only, deliberately. A key supplied by the browser would be a\npath-traversal and cross-tenant-write vector."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prompt-media-referenced-by-url","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prompt media referenced by URL"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What gets stored is the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generated output"}]},{"type":"text","value":". When a provider returns an\nexpiring link, the middleware downloads it and keeps the bytes, which is the\nwhole point of this page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prompt media is different, and it splits by how you sent it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"base64"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source: { type: 'data' }"},{"type":"text","value":"): stored alongside the output, because\nthe bytes are already in hand."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a URL"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source: { type: 'url' }"},{"type":"text","value":"): "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not fetched"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and no artifact is\nrecorded for it."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two reasons a caller-supplied URL is left alone. Downloading it server-side\nwould let anyone name an address your server can reach (cloud metadata\nendpoints, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" admin services) and then read the response back through\nthe artifact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" route. The copy is also redundant: whoever supplied the URL\nalready had the media."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you do need a durable copy of caller-supplied media (a \"paste an image URL\"\ninput box, say), opt in with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowInputUrl"},{"type":"text","value":", which is a predicate rather than a\nflag precisely so the check is not optional:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const inputUrlOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  allowInputUrl: ({ url }) => url.hostname.endsWith('.cdn.example.com'),\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every artifact fetch, input or output, is bounded three ways:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The scheme must be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"http:"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"https:"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is aborted after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactFetchTimeoutMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 30s)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is capped at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxArtifactBytes"},{"type":"text","value":" (default 1 GiB) as the body drains, or not\nat all when you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"; see "},{"type":"link","href":"#nothing-is-buffered","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing is buffered"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Input fetches add two more: a loopback / private / link-local host block, and a\nrefusal to follow redirects, so a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"302"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot hop somewhere the check never\nsaw."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat those as a backstop, not the control: a hostname that "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"resolves"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to a\nprivate address still passes a literal-IP check. Keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowInputUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" narrow,\nand for stronger isolation inject "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactFetch"},{"type":"text","value":" to route downloads through an\negress-restricted proxy that can check the address actually connected to."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"nothing-is-buffered","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing is buffered"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A provider URL is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"streamed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" into the blob store: the middleware never holds\nthe artifact in memory, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":" on the record is counted as the bytes drain.\nA 2 GB video costs a streaming store (R2, S3, a filesystem) the same memory as\na 2 KB icon. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" is the exception, and only because holding the\nbytes in process "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"is"}]},{"type":"text","value":" what it does: it is a dev/test store, not a production\none."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxArtifactBytes"},{"type":"text","value":" is therefore a bound on "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"transfer"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not on memory. What it\nbuys is a ceiling on what a runaway or hostile origin can make you pull and\nstore: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-length"},{"type":"text","value":" is advisory, so an origin can declare 1 KB and send\nforever. That is the only reason there is a default at all."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"a-provider-stream-is-stored-the-same-way","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A provider stream is stored the same way"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some video providers return the finished video as a stream, with no URL to\nfetch. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Videos with no public URL"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe middleware passes that stream to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore.put"},{"type":"text","value":" as it is, so memory stays\nflat here too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two differences from a fetched URL:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No declared length."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The stream has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-length"},{"type":"text","value":", and the\nmiddleware sends no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobPutOptions.expectedLength"},{"type":"text","value":". Your store must accept\na stream of unknown length. On R2 that means a multipart upload."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No size cap."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxArtifactBytes"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to URL fetches only."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-body-reaches-your-store-untouched-when-it-can","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The body reaches your store untouched when it can"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enforcing the cap during the drain means wrapping the body in a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformStream"},{"type":"text","value":" that counts, and a transform's readable side carries "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no\ndeclared length"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which is exactly what workerd's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"R2Bucket.put"},{"type":"text","value":" needs for a\nsingle-shot upload. So the wrapper is applied only where it is load-bearing:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The response"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What your store gets"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"declares a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-length"},{"type":"text","value":", no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-encoding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" body "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"untouched"}]},{"type":"text","value":", length intact"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chunked, no declared length"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the counting wrapper"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content-encoding: gzip"},{"type":"text","value":" (declared length is compressed)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the counting wrapper"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the first row nothing is lost by skipping the counter: the declared length\nwas already checked against the cap, and HTTP framing holds the origin to it,\na body cannot exceed a length it declared. That is the common case for a\nprovider CDN, so on Cloudflare the default configuration already streams\nstraight through:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Inside your BlobStore.put on workerd: nothing buffered, no multipart, no\n// hint needed. (`R2Bucket` comes from @cloudflare/workers-types.)\nconst putStraightToR2 = (bucket: R2Bucket, key: string, body: BlobBody) =>\n  bucket.put(key, body)","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The other two rows genuinely need the counter, because a chunked reply declares\nnothing, and a compressed one can decode to arbitrarily more than it declared\n(a decompression bomb). For those, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobPutOptions.expectedLength"},{"type":"text","value":" is absent\ntoo, and a length-strict store falls back to a multipart upload that buffers\none 8 MiB part at a time, so memory stays flat whatever the artifact's size. The\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store"},{"type":"text","value":" skill ships that recipe."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To drop the ceiling entirely (no counter on any response, no limit on what an\norigin can stream into your bucket) pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const uncappedOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  maxArtifactBytes: false,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=generation-bytes"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reach for that when you trust the origins you fetch from and would rather have\nno ceiling than a generous one. Your storage backend's own limits still apply,\nR2, for instance, caps a single-shot upload at 5 GiB and a multipart one at\n10,000 parts. Keep the cap when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowInputUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" lets callers name the URL:\nthere the origin is by definition not one you control."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wire-the-durable-url-through-to-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire the durable URL through to the client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifactUrl"},{"type":"text","value":" is what makes the stored bytes reachable from the client without\nany extra plumbing. For each persisted ref it returns the app-origin URL that\nserves those bytes (the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" route above), and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" does\ntwo things with it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stamps the URL onto the ref, as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ref.url"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewrites the live result's media field to the same URL: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.images[i].url"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor images, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.url"},{"type":"text","value":" for a video, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.audio.url"},{"type":"text","value":" for audio."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So the live result already points at your origin, not the provider's expiring\nlink."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Those durable refs ride along on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":", and they are what a reload\nrestores from. In "},{"type":"link","href":"./generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the\ngeneration hook rebuilds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result"},{"type":"text","value":" from the persisted refs on mount, resolving\neach media field to its durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ref.url"},{"type":"text","value":", so the restored result renders the\nsame media the live run showed. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" is the whole artifact surface\non the hook: there are no separate top-level artifact fields to read, live or\nrestored."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to go next"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the run record that\nsurvives a reload or a dropped connection, and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" store that\nbyte storage builds on."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-generation-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a generation adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a\ncustom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ArtifactStore"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BlobStore"},{"type":"text","value":" on your own database."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"serve-the-stored-bytes","text":"Serve the stored bytes","level":2},{"id":"serve-video-honour-range","text":"Serve video: honour Range","level":3},{"id":"choose-where-the-bytes-land","text":"Choose where the bytes land","level":2},{"id":"prompt-media-referenced-by-url","text":"Prompt media referenced by URL","level":2},{"id":"nothing-is-buffered","text":"Nothing is buffered","level":2},{"id":"a-provider-stream-is-stored-the-same-way","text":"A provider stream is stored the same way","level":3},{"id":"the-body-reaches-your-store-untouched-when-it-can","text":"The body reaches your store untouched when it can","level":3},{"id":"wire-the-durable-url-through-to-the-client","text":"Wire the durable URL through to the client","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nProvider URLs for generated media expire. A Sora clip, a batch of images, a long\naudio track: the model hands you a URL that stops working after a while, and\nonce it does the output is gone. To keep the output, save the generated bytes to\nyour own storage and serve them from your own origin, where they outlive the\nprovider's link.\n\nThis is a server-side opt-in that layers on **top of** the `generationRuns` store\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence) already requires. Byte storage\nadds two more stores, which must be provided together:\n\n- an `artifacts` store: the metadata.\n- a `blobs` store: the bytes.\n\nWhat each choice gets you:\n\n- **Both provided**: `withGenerationPersistence` writes each generated file's\n  bytes to the blob store, records an `ArtifactRecord`, and attaches durable\n  references to the result and the run record.\n- **Neither provided**: only the run record is kept.\n\n`memoryPersistence()` ships all three stores (`generationRuns`, `artifacts`,\n`blobs`), so it works out of the box. Any backend that implements `ArtifactStore`\nand `BlobStore` (see\n[Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter))\nworks the same way.\n\n## Serve the stored bytes\n\nThe bytes land under the blob key `artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`. To fetch a\ngenerated file later (to render it, download it, or hand it to another request),\nadd a `GET` route that reads the artifact back with the `retrieveArtifact` /\n`retrieveBlob` helpers and streams it from your own origin:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\n// routes/api.generate.image.ts, runs the generation.\nimport {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  retrieveArtifact,\n  retrieveBlob,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest(\n    'image',\n    request,\n  )\n\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  // Persistence requires the scope these runs are filed under.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [\n      withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n        // Stamp the durable serve URL (the artifact route below) onto every\n        // persisted artifact ref, and rewrite the live result's media field to\n        // it. Both the live and the restored result then render from your own\n        // origin instead of the provider's expiring link.\n        artifactUrl: (ref) =>\n          `/api/generate/image/artifact?id=${ref.artifactId}`,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe serve route is a **separate** route from the generation endpoint. A `GET` on\nthe generation route is already spoken for by\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence), which is where a reloading\nclient hydrates and where an in-flight run resumes, so the bytes get their own\npath, the one `artifactUrl` stamps above:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\n// routes/api.generate.image.artifact.ts, serves stored bytes by id.\n//\n// This is a plain file endpoint: it serves one stored file, it does not resume\n// a run or rebuild a conversation.\n//\n// Security: the id comes from the caller, so this route MUST authorize before\n// it serves. `ArtifactRecord` carries the `threadId` / `runId` the file was\n// generated under. Check that against an identity you derive server-side from\n// the session, never from the query string. Without this check, any caller who\n// learns or guesses an artifact id can read another user's media.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const artifactId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('id')\n  if (!artifactId) return new Response('missing id', { status: 400 })\n\n  const artifact = await retrieveArtifact(persistence, artifactId)\n  if (!artifact) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  // Replace with your session + ownership check, e.g.:\n  // const user = await auth(request)\n  // const owned = user != null && (await db.threadOwnedBy(user.id, artifact.threadId))\n  const owned = true\n  void request\n  // 404, not 403. A distinguishable \"exists but forbidden\" confirms valid ids.\n  if (!owned) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(persistence, artifact)\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  return new Response(blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer()), {\n    headers: {\n      'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n      'content-length': String(artifact.size),\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Serve video: honour `Range`\n\nThe route above is enough for images. Video is not: seeking a `<video>` is\nbuilt on HTTP range requests, and a source that answers every `Range` with the\nwhole file cannot be scrubbed: Safari refuses to play it at all, and every\nother browser downloads the entire clip before it starts. Pass `range` to\n`retrieveBlob` and answer `206`:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nimport { parseRangeHeader } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ArtifactRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\n// The same route, seek-aware. `parseRangeHeader` resolves the header against\n// the size on the record, including suffix ranges (`bytes=-500` is the LAST\n// 500 bytes) and the unsatisfiable case. `blob.range` then reports the slice\n// actually served, and `artifact.size` the whole file: the two numbers\n// `Content-Range` needs.\nexport async function serveArtifactBytes(\n  request: Request,\n  artifact: ArtifactRecord,\n) {\n  const range = parseRangeHeader(request.headers.get('range'), artifact.size)\n  if (range === 'unsatisfiable') {\n    return new Response('range not satisfiable', {\n      status: 416,\n      headers: { 'content-range': `bytes */${artifact.size}` },\n    })\n  }\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(\n    persistence,\n    artifact,\n    range ? { range } : undefined,\n  )\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const body = blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer())\n  // `accept-ranges` on every response, including the whole-file one: it is how\n  // the player learns it may seek at all.\n  const headers = {\n    'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n    'accept-ranges': 'bytes',\n  }\n  if (!blob.range) {\n    return new Response(body, {\n      headers: { ...headers, 'content-length': String(artifact.size) },\n    })\n  }\n  const { offset, length } = blob.range\n  return new Response(body, {\n    status: 206,\n    headers: {\n      ...headers,\n      'content-length': String(length),\n      'content-range': `bytes ${offset}-${offset + length - 1}/${artifact.size}`,\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe client side needs nothing special, which is the point. Given a route that\nanswers ranges, the browser drives the rest:\n\n```tsx\nimport type { PersistedArtifactRef } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function GeneratedVideo({ artifact }: { artifact: PersistedArtifactRef }) {\n  // `artifact.url` is the app-origin URL `artifactUrl` stamped on the ref.\n  return <video src={artifact.url} controls preload=\"metadata\" />\n}\n```\n\n`preload=\"metadata\"` has the player fetch the header bytes with a range request\nand show the duration and scrubber without pulling the clip down. A store must\nsupport ranged reads for any of this to work; the\n[conformance testkit](./store-reference#blobstore) asserts it.\n\n`memoryPersistence` keeps everything in process memory, which is right for\ndevelopment and tests; point `generationRuns` / `artifacts` / `blobs` at a durable backend\nfor production. Control what gets captured with `withGenerationPersistence`'s\n`extractArtifacts` (return your own descriptors) and `nameArtifact` (name each\nfile) options.\n\nA custom `extractArtifacts` replaces the built-in rule. If you use a video\nprovider with [no public URL](../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url),\nreturn a descriptor with `bytes: result.body` for the video. If you do not, the\nstream is not stored and `url` is a base64 `data:` URL.\n\n## Choose where the bytes land\n\nBy default an artifact's bytes are written under\n`artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`. Pass `storageKey` to put them in your own\nfolder structure instead, which helps when the bucket is shared with the rest of\nyour app, or when you want media grouped by the thing it belongs to rather than\nby the run that produced it:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst storageKeyOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  storageKey: ({ runId, artifactId, role, name }) =>\n    `products/${role}/${runId}-${artifactId}-${name}`,\n})\n```\n\nTwo things worth knowing:\n\n**The resolved key is recorded on the artifact.** Once the path is arbitrary it\ncan no longer be recomputed from the record, so it is stored as\n`ArtifactRecord.blobKey` and reads resolve through it. Records written before\nthis existed fall back to the default convention, so adding `storageKey` to an\napp with existing artifacts does not orphan them. It does mean the default\nconvention can never be changed retroactively.\n\n**Returning a non-unique key overwrites.** Include `artifactId`, or something\nequally unique, unless overwriting is what you want.\n\nThis is server-side only, deliberately. A key supplied by the browser would be a\npath-traversal and cross-tenant-write vector.\n\n## Prompt media referenced by URL\n\nWhat gets stored is the **generated output**. When a provider returns an\nexpiring link, the middleware downloads it and keeps the bytes, which is the\nwhole point of this page.\n\nPrompt media is different, and it splits by how you sent it:\n\n- **base64** (`source: { type: 'data' }`): stored alongside the output, because\n  the bytes are already in hand.\n- **a URL** (`source: { type: 'url' }`): **not fetched**, and no artifact is\n  recorded for it.\n\nTwo reasons a caller-supplied URL is left alone. Downloading it server-side\nwould let anyone name an address your server can reach (cloud metadata\nendpoints, `localhost` admin services) and then read the response back through\nthe artifact `GET` route. The copy is also redundant: whoever supplied the URL\nalready had the media.\n\nIf you do need a durable copy of caller-supplied media (a \"paste an image URL\"\ninput box, say), opt in with `allowInputUrl`, which is a predicate rather than a\nflag precisely so the check is not optional:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst inputUrlOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  allowInputUrl: ({ url }) => url.hostname.endsWith('.cdn.example.com'),\n})\n```\n\nEvery artifact fetch, input or output, is bounded three ways:\n\n- The scheme must be `http:` or `https:`.\n- It is aborted after `artifactFetchTimeoutMs` (default 30s).\n- It is capped at `maxArtifactBytes` (default 1 GiB) as the body drains, or not\n  at all when you pass `false`; see [Nothing is buffered](#nothing-is-buffered).\n\nInput fetches add two more: a loopback / private / link-local host block, and a\nrefusal to follow redirects, so a `302` cannot hop somewhere the check never\nsaw.\n\nTreat those as a backstop, not the control: a hostname that *resolves* to a\nprivate address still passes a literal-IP check. Keep `allowInputUrl` narrow,\nand for stronger isolation inject `artifactFetch` to route downloads through an\negress-restricted proxy that can check the address actually connected to.\n\n## Nothing is buffered\n\nA provider URL is **streamed** into the blob store: the middleware never holds\nthe artifact in memory, and `size` on the record is counted as the bytes drain.\nA 2 GB video costs a streaming store (R2, S3, a filesystem) the same memory as\na 2 KB icon. `memoryPersistence` is the exception, and only because holding the\nbytes in process *is* what it does: it is a dev/test store, not a production\none.\n\n`maxArtifactBytes` is therefore a bound on **transfer**, not on memory. What it\nbuys is a ceiling on what a runaway or hostile origin can make you pull and\nstore: `content-length` is advisory, so an origin can declare 1 KB and send\nforever. That is the only reason there is a default at all.\n\n### A provider stream is stored the same way\n\nSome video providers return the finished video as a stream, with no URL to\nfetch. See [Videos with no public URL](../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url).\nThe middleware passes that stream to `BlobStore.put` as it is, so memory stays\nflat here too.\n\nTwo differences from a fetched URL:\n\n- **No declared length.** The stream has no `content-length`, and the\n  middleware sends no `BlobPutOptions.expectedLength`. Your store must accept\n  a stream of unknown length. On R2 that means a multipart upload.\n- **No size cap.** `maxArtifactBytes` applies to URL fetches only.\n\n### The body reaches your store untouched when it can\n\nEnforcing the cap during the drain means wrapping the body in a\n`TransformStream` that counts, and a transform's readable side carries **no\ndeclared length**, which is exactly what workerd's `R2Bucket.put` needs for a\nsingle-shot upload. So the wrapper is applied only where it is load-bearing:\n\n| The response                                            | What your store gets                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| declares a `content-length`, no `content-encoding`       | the `fetch` body **untouched**, length intact |\n| chunked, no declared length                              | the counting wrapper                        |\n| `content-encoding: gzip` (declared length is compressed) | the counting wrapper                        |\n\nIn the first row nothing is lost by skipping the counter: the declared length\nwas already checked against the cap, and HTTP framing holds the origin to it,\na body cannot exceed a length it declared. That is the common case for a\nprovider CDN, so on Cloudflare the default configuration already streams\nstraight through:\n\n```ts ignore\n// Inside your BlobStore.put on workerd: nothing buffered, no multipart, no\n// hint needed. (`R2Bucket` comes from @cloudflare/workers-types.)\nconst putStraightToR2 = (bucket: R2Bucket, key: string, body: BlobBody) =>\n  bucket.put(key, body)\n```\n\nThe other two rows genuinely need the counter, because a chunked reply declares\nnothing, and a compressed one can decode to arbitrarily more than it declared\n(a decompression bomb). For those, `BlobPutOptions.expectedLength` is absent\ntoo, and a length-strict store falls back to a multipart upload that buffers\none 8 MiB part at a time, so memory stays flat whatever the artifact's size. The\n`ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store` skill ships that recipe.\n\nTo drop the ceiling entirely (no counter on any response, no limit on what an\norigin can stream into your bucket) pass `false`:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst uncappedOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  maxArtifactBytes: false,\n})\n```\n\nReach for that when you trust the origins you fetch from and would rather have\nno ceiling than a generous one. Your storage backend's own limits still apply,\nR2, for instance, caps a single-shot upload at 5 GiB and a multipart one at\n10,000 parts. Keep the cap when `allowInputUrl` lets callers name the URL:\nthere the origin is by definition not one you control.\n\n## Wire the durable URL through to the client\n\n`artifactUrl` is what makes the stored bytes reachable from the client without\nany extra plumbing. For each persisted ref it returns the app-origin URL that\nserves those bytes (the `GET` route above), and `withGenerationPersistence` does\ntwo things with it:\n\n- Stamps the URL onto the ref, as `ref.url`.\n- Rewrites the live result's media field to the same URL: `result.images[i].url`\n  for images, `result.url` for a video, `result.audio.url` for audio.\n\nSo the live result already points at your origin, not the provider's expiring\nlink.\n\nThose durable refs ride along on `result.artifacts`, and they are what a reload\nrestores from. In [Generation persistence](./generation-persistence), the\ngeneration hook rebuilds `result` from the persisted refs on mount, resolving\neach media field to its durable `ref.url`, so the restored result renders the\nsame media the live run showed. `result.artifacts` is the whole artifact surface\non the hook: there are no separate top-level artifact fields to read, live or\nrestored.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Generation persistence](./generation-persistence): the run record that\n  survives a reload or a dropped connection, and the `generationRuns` store that\n  byte storage builds on.\n- [Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter): a\n  custom `ArtifactStore` / `BlobStore` on your own database.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Keep Generated Files (Advanced)\nid: keep-generated-files\n---\n\nProvider URLs for generated media expire. A Sora clip, a batch of images, a long\naudio track: the model hands you a URL that stops working after a while, and\nonce it does the output is gone. To keep the output, save the generated bytes to\nyour own storage and serve them from your own origin, where they outlive the\nprovider's link.\n\nThis is a server-side opt-in that layers on **top of** the `generationRuns` store\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence) already requires. Byte storage\nadds two more stores, which must be provided together:\n\n- an `artifacts` store: the metadata.\n- a `blobs` store: the bytes.\n\nWhat each choice gets you:\n\n- **Both provided**: `withGenerationPersistence` writes each generated file's\n  bytes to the blob store, records an `ArtifactRecord`, and attaches durable\n  references to the result and the run record.\n- **Neither provided**: only the run record is kept.\n\n`memoryPersistence()` ships all three stores (`generationRuns`, `artifacts`,\n`blobs`), so it works out of the box. Any backend that implements `ArtifactStore`\nand `BlobStore` (see\n[Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter))\nworks the same way.\n\n## Serve the stored bytes\n\nThe bytes land under the blob key `artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`. To fetch a\ngenerated file later (to render it, download it, or hand it to another request),\nadd a `GET` route that reads the artifact back with the `retrieveArtifact` /\n`retrieveBlob` helpers and streams it from your own origin:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\n// routes/api.generate.image.ts, runs the generation.\nimport {\n  generateImage,\n  generationParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  memoryPersistence,\n  retrieveArtifact,\n  retrieveBlob,\n  withGenerationPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { input, threadId } = await generationParamsFromRequest(\n    'image',\n    request,\n  )\n\n  if (typeof input.prompt !== 'string') {\n    throw new Error('This endpoint accepts text image prompts only.')\n  }\n\n  // Persistence requires the scope these runs are filed under.\n  if (threadId === undefined) {\n    return new Response('`threadId` is required', { status: 400 })\n  }\n\n  const stream = generateImage({\n    adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n    prompt: input.prompt,\n    threadId,\n    stream: true,\n    middleware: [\n      withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n        // Stamp the durable serve URL (the artifact route below) onto every\n        // persisted artifact ref, and rewrite the live result's media field to\n        // it. Both the live and the restored result then render from your own\n        // origin instead of the provider's expiring link.\n        artifactUrl: (ref) =>\n          `/api/generate/image/artifact?id=${ref.artifactId}`,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe serve route is a **separate** route from the generation endpoint. A `GET` on\nthe generation route is already spoken for by\n[Generation persistence](./generation-persistence), which is where a reloading\nclient hydrates and where an in-flight run resumes, so the bytes get their own\npath, the one `artifactUrl` stamps above:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\n// routes/api.generate.image.artifact.ts, serves stored bytes by id.\n//\n// This is a plain file endpoint: it serves one stored file, it does not resume\n// a run or rebuild a conversation.\n//\n// Security: the id comes from the caller, so this route MUST authorize before\n// it serves. `ArtifactRecord` carries the `threadId` / `runId` the file was\n// generated under. Check that against an identity you derive server-side from\n// the session, never from the query string. Without this check, any caller who\n// learns or guesses an artifact id can read another user's media.\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  const artifactId = new URL(request.url).searchParams.get('id')\n  if (!artifactId) return new Response('missing id', { status: 400 })\n\n  const artifact = await retrieveArtifact(persistence, artifactId)\n  if (!artifact) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  // Replace with your session + ownership check, e.g.:\n  // const user = await auth(request)\n  // const owned = user != null && (await db.threadOwnedBy(user.id, artifact.threadId))\n  const owned = true\n  void request\n  // 404, not 403. A distinguishable \"exists but forbidden\" confirms valid ids.\n  if (!owned) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(persistence, artifact)\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  return new Response(blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer()), {\n    headers: {\n      'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n      'content-length': String(artifact.size),\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\n### Serve video: honour `Range`\n\nThe route above is enough for images. Video is not: seeking a `<video>` is\nbuilt on HTTP range requests, and a source that answers every `Range` with the\nwhole file cannot be scrubbed: Safari refuses to play it at all, and every\nother browser downloads the entire clip before it starts. Pass `range` to\n`retrieveBlob` and answer `206`:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nimport { parseRangeHeader } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ArtifactRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\n// The same route, seek-aware. `parseRangeHeader` resolves the header against\n// the size on the record, including suffix ranges (`bytes=-500` is the LAST\n// 500 bytes) and the unsatisfiable case. `blob.range` then reports the slice\n// actually served, and `artifact.size` the whole file: the two numbers\n// `Content-Range` needs.\nexport async function serveArtifactBytes(\n  request: Request,\n  artifact: ArtifactRecord,\n) {\n  const range = parseRangeHeader(request.headers.get('range'), artifact.size)\n  if (range === 'unsatisfiable') {\n    return new Response('range not satisfiable', {\n      status: 416,\n      headers: { 'content-range': `bytes */${artifact.size}` },\n    })\n  }\n\n  const blob = await retrieveBlob(\n    persistence,\n    artifact,\n    range ? { range } : undefined,\n  )\n  if (!blob) return new Response('not found', { status: 404 })\n\n  const body = blob.body ?? (await blob.arrayBuffer())\n  // `accept-ranges` on every response, including the whole-file one: it is how\n  // the player learns it may seek at all.\n  const headers = {\n    'content-type': artifact.mimeType,\n    'accept-ranges': 'bytes',\n  }\n  if (!blob.range) {\n    return new Response(body, {\n      headers: { ...headers, 'content-length': String(artifact.size) },\n    })\n  }\n  const { offset, length } = blob.range\n  return new Response(body, {\n    status: 206,\n    headers: {\n      ...headers,\n      'content-length': String(length),\n      'content-range': `bytes ${offset}-${offset + length - 1}/${artifact.size}`,\n    },\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe client side needs nothing special, which is the point. Given a route that\nanswers ranges, the browser drives the rest:\n\n```tsx\nimport type { PersistedArtifactRef } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nexport function GeneratedVideo({ artifact }: { artifact: PersistedArtifactRef }) {\n  // `artifact.url` is the app-origin URL `artifactUrl` stamped on the ref.\n  return <video src={artifact.url} controls preload=\"metadata\" />\n}\n```\n\n`preload=\"metadata\"` has the player fetch the header bytes with a range request\nand show the duration and scrubber without pulling the clip down. A store must\nsupport ranged reads for any of this to work; the\n[conformance testkit](./store-reference#blobstore) asserts it.\n\n`memoryPersistence` keeps everything in process memory, which is right for\ndevelopment and tests; point `generationRuns` / `artifacts` / `blobs` at a durable backend\nfor production. Control what gets captured with `withGenerationPersistence`'s\n`extractArtifacts` (return your own descriptors) and `nameArtifact` (name each\nfile) options.\n\nA custom `extractArtifacts` replaces the built-in rule. If you use a video\nprovider with [no public URL](../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url),\nreturn a descriptor with `bytes: result.body` for the video. If you do not, the\nstream is not stored and `url` is a base64 `data:` URL.\n\n## Choose where the bytes land\n\nBy default an artifact's bytes are written under\n`artifacts/<runId>/<artifactId>`. Pass `storageKey` to put them in your own\nfolder structure instead, which helps when the bucket is shared with the rest of\nyour app, or when you want media grouped by the thing it belongs to rather than\nby the run that produced it:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst storageKeyOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  storageKey: ({ runId, artifactId, role, name }) =>\n    `products/${role}/${runId}-${artifactId}-${name}`,\n})\n```\n\nTwo things worth knowing:\n\n**The resolved key is recorded on the artifact.** Once the path is arbitrary it\ncan no longer be recomputed from the record, so it is stored as\n`ArtifactRecord.blobKey` and reads resolve through it. Records written before\nthis existed fall back to the default convention, so adding `storageKey` to an\napp with existing artifacts does not orphan them. It does mean the default\nconvention can never be changed retroactively.\n\n**Returning a non-unique key overwrites.** Include `artifactId`, or something\nequally unique, unless overwriting is what you want.\n\nThis is server-side only, deliberately. A key supplied by the browser would be a\npath-traversal and cross-tenant-write vector.\n\n## Prompt media referenced by URL\n\nWhat gets stored is the **generated output**. When a provider returns an\nexpiring link, the middleware downloads it and keeps the bytes, which is the\nwhole point of this page.\n\nPrompt media is different, and it splits by how you sent it:\n\n- **base64** (`source: { type: 'data' }`): stored alongside the output, because\n  the bytes are already in hand.\n- **a URL** (`source: { type: 'url' }`): **not fetched**, and no artifact is\n  recorded for it.\n\nTwo reasons a caller-supplied URL is left alone. Downloading it server-side\nwould let anyone name an address your server can reach (cloud metadata\nendpoints, `localhost` admin services) and then read the response back through\nthe artifact `GET` route. The copy is also redundant: whoever supplied the URL\nalready had the media.\n\nIf you do need a durable copy of caller-supplied media (a \"paste an image URL\"\ninput box, say), opt in with `allowInputUrl`, which is a predicate rather than a\nflag precisely so the check is not optional:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst inputUrlOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  allowInputUrl: ({ url }) => url.hostname.endsWith('.cdn.example.com'),\n})\n```\n\nEvery artifact fetch, input or output, is bounded three ways:\n\n- The scheme must be `http:` or `https:`.\n- It is aborted after `artifactFetchTimeoutMs` (default 30s).\n- It is capped at `maxArtifactBytes` (default 1 GiB) as the body drains, or not\n  at all when you pass `false`; see [Nothing is buffered](#nothing-is-buffered).\n\nInput fetches add two more: a loopback / private / link-local host block, and a\nrefusal to follow redirects, so a `302` cannot hop somewhere the check never\nsaw.\n\nTreat those as a backstop, not the control: a hostname that *resolves* to a\nprivate address still passes a literal-IP check. Keep `allowInputUrl` narrow,\nand for stronger isolation inject `artifactFetch` to route downloads through an\negress-restricted proxy that can check the address actually connected to.\n\n## Nothing is buffered\n\nA provider URL is **streamed** into the blob store: the middleware never holds\nthe artifact in memory, and `size` on the record is counted as the bytes drain.\nA 2 GB video costs a streaming store (R2, S3, a filesystem) the same memory as\na 2 KB icon. `memoryPersistence` is the exception, and only because holding the\nbytes in process *is* what it does: it is a dev/test store, not a production\none.\n\n`maxArtifactBytes` is therefore a bound on **transfer**, not on memory. What it\nbuys is a ceiling on what a runaway or hostile origin can make you pull and\nstore: `content-length` is advisory, so an origin can declare 1 KB and send\nforever. That is the only reason there is a default at all.\n\n### A provider stream is stored the same way\n\nSome video providers return the finished video as a stream, with no URL to\nfetch. See [Videos with no public URL](../media/video-generation#videos-with-no-public-url).\nThe middleware passes that stream to `BlobStore.put` as it is, so memory stays\nflat here too.\n\nTwo differences from a fetched URL:\n\n- **No declared length.** The stream has no `content-length`, and the\n  middleware sends no `BlobPutOptions.expectedLength`. Your store must accept\n  a stream of unknown length. On R2 that means a multipart upload.\n- **No size cap.** `maxArtifactBytes` applies to URL fetches only.\n\n### The body reaches your store untouched when it can\n\nEnforcing the cap during the drain means wrapping the body in a\n`TransformStream` that counts, and a transform's readable side carries **no\ndeclared length**, which is exactly what workerd's `R2Bucket.put` needs for a\nsingle-shot upload. So the wrapper is applied only where it is load-bearing:\n\n| The response                                            | What your store gets                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| declares a `content-length`, no `content-encoding`       | the `fetch` body **untouched**, length intact |\n| chunked, no declared length                              | the counting wrapper                        |\n| `content-encoding: gzip` (declared length is compressed) | the counting wrapper                        |\n\nIn the first row nothing is lost by skipping the counter: the declared length\nwas already checked against the cap, and HTTP framing holds the origin to it,\na body cannot exceed a length it declared. That is the common case for a\nprovider CDN, so on Cloudflare the default configuration already streams\nstraight through:\n\n```ts ignore\n// Inside your BlobStore.put on workerd: nothing buffered, no multipart, no\n// hint needed. (`R2Bucket` comes from @cloudflare/workers-types.)\nconst putStraightToR2 = (bucket: R2Bucket, key: string, body: BlobBody) =>\n  bucket.put(key, body)\n```\n\nThe other two rows genuinely need the counter, because a chunked reply declares\nnothing, and a compressed one can decode to arbitrarily more than it declared\n(a decompression bomb). For those, `BlobPutOptions.expectedLength` is absent\ntoo, and a length-strict store falls back to a multipart upload that buffers\none 8 MiB part at a time, so memory stays flat whatever the artifact's size. The\n`ai-persistence/build-cloudflare-artifact-store` skill ships that recipe.\n\nTo drop the ceiling entirely (no counter on any response, no limit on what an\norigin can stream into your bucket) pass `false`:\n\n```ts group=generation-bytes\nconst uncappedOptions = withGenerationPersistence(persistence, {\n  maxArtifactBytes: false,\n})\n```\n\nReach for that when you trust the origins you fetch from and would rather have\nno ceiling than a generous one. Your storage backend's own limits still apply,\nR2, for instance, caps a single-shot upload at 5 GiB and a multipart one at\n10,000 parts. Keep the cap when `allowInputUrl` lets callers name the URL:\nthere the origin is by definition not one you control.\n\n## Wire the durable URL through to the client\n\n`artifactUrl` is what makes the stored bytes reachable from the client without\nany extra plumbing. For each persisted ref it returns the app-origin URL that\nserves those bytes (the `GET` route above), and `withGenerationPersistence` does\ntwo things with it:\n\n- Stamps the URL onto the ref, as `ref.url`.\n- Rewrites the live result's media field to the same URL: `result.images[i].url`\n  for images, `result.url` for a video, `result.audio.url` for audio.\n\nSo the live result already points at your origin, not the provider's expiring\nlink.\n\nThose durable refs ride along on `result.artifacts`, and they are what a reload\nrestores from. In [Generation persistence](./generation-persistence), the\ngeneration hook rebuilds `result` from the persisted refs on mount, resolving\neach media field to its durable `ref.url`, so the restored result renders the\nsame media the live run showed. `result.artifacts` is the whole artifact surface\non the hook: there are no separate top-level artifact fields to read, live or\nrestored.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Generation persistence](./generation-persistence): the run record that\n  survives a reload or a dropped connection, and the `generationRuns` store that\n  byte storage builds on.\n- [Build a generation adapter](./build-your-own-generation-adapter): a\n  custom `ArtifactStore` / `BlobStore` on your own database.\n"}
